Dialogue: 0,0:00:01.12,0:00:05.10,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,160)}Water enters a plant through\Nits root hair cells by osmosis,
Dialogue: 0,0:00:05.14,0:00:07.64,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,160)}moving across selectively permeable membranes
Dialogue: 0,0:00:07.68,0:00:10.28,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,160)}from higher to lower water potential.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:10.72,0:00:13.34,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,160)}It then travels through the root into the xylem,
Dialogue: 0,0:00:13.38,0:00:17.74,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,160)}where a continuous column of liquid water\Nextends upward toward the leaves.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:18.12,0:00:20.32,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,160)}Inside the leaf, water evaporates
Dialogue: 0,0:00:20.36,0:00:24.42,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,160)}from the moist walls of\Nmesophyll cells into tiny air spaces.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:24.62,0:00:29.42,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,160)}The resulting water vapour diffuses\Nthrough open stomata into the atmosphere.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:29.87,0:00:33.39,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,160)}Evaporation creates tension, or negative pressure,
Dialogue: 0,0:00:33.43,0:00:37.79,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,160)}which pulls the remaining water upward\Nthrough the xylem, against gravity.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:38.17,0:00:42.83,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,160)}Cohesion, caused largely by\Nhydrogen bonds between water molecules,
Dialogue: 0,0:00:42.95,0:00:45.41,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,160)}helps maintain the continuous water column.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:45.50,0:00:48.86,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,160)}Adhesion attracts water molecules\Nto the xylem walls.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:49.32,0:00:51.36,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,160)}Guard cells regulate the stomata,
Dialogue: 0,0:00:51.40,0:00:56.32,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,160)}balancing carbon dioxide uptake for\Nphotosynthesis against water loss.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:56.72,0:01:00.16,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,160)}Together, these processes\Nproduce the transpiration stream,
Dialogue: 0,0:01:00.20,0:01:02.38,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,160)}carrying water from roots to leaves.
